 ## Overview
-Simplex Noise is a gradient-based procedural noise function that generates smooth, continuous scalar fields in 2D, 3D, and 4D space [S1, unofficial]. It solves the directional artifacts inherent in earlier grid-based noise functions (like Perlin Noise) by using a skewed simplex grid (triangles in 2D, tetrahedra in 3D) instead of a cubic grid, which reduces computational complexity and improves isotropy [S2, unofficial]. It is primarily used for terrain generation, texture synthesis, and as a density function $f(x,y,z)$ for implicit surfaces or voxel terrain [S3, unofficial].
+Simplex Noise is a gradient-based procedural noise function that generates smooth, continuous scalar fields by evaluating dot products between input coordinates and pseudo-random gradient vectors assigned to simplex cell vertices [S1, S3, S5, unofficial]. It solves the directional artifacts and computational inefficiency of cubic-grid Perlin noise by using a skewed simplex (triangular in 2D, tetrahedral in 3D) grid, which reduces the number of gradient evaluations and dot products required per sample [S1, S2, S3, unofficial][S6]. Unlike its sibling [Simplex Noise 2D](4155-simplex-noise-2d.md) which operates in 2D, this 3D variant specifically optimizes for volumetric terrain and texture generation by reducing the number of gradient evaluations and dot products required per sample [S2, unofficial].
 
